---
/**
 * OpenGov Chart of Accounts Manager case study overlay (Figma node 401:3722).
 *
 * The long-form variant: prose sections with supporting assets, a banner out to
 * a live prototype, and a closing pair of images. Opened by the `sun` carousel
 * card.
 *
 * Header, copy, and hero come from the registry entry so this study reads the
 * same on the card and in the overlay; only the body blocks live here. The
 * design's byline reads "Google | Summer 2026"; the registry keeps the
 * carousel's "OpenGov | 2016".
 *
 * NOTE: the design reuses the same placeholder paragraph under "Usability
 * testing", "Working with Customer Success", and "Impact"; that is carried over
 * verbatim here and still needs real copy.
 */
import CaseStudyOverlay from "../CaseStudyOverlay.astro";
import ProseBlock from "../case-study/ProseBlock.astro";
import AssetRow from "../case-study/AssetRow.astro";
import Banner from "../case-study/Banner.astro";
import { chartOfAccounts, toOverlayProps } from "../../lib/case-studies";

import financeDirector from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/finance-director.png";
import livePrototype from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/live-prototype.png";
import opengovReport from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/opengov-report.png";
import animalTaxonomy from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/animal-taxonomy.png";
import oldCoaEditor from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/old-coa-editor.png";
import diagrams from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/diagrams.png";
import earlyConcept from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/early-concept.png";
import artifact from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/artifact.png";
import slotMachine from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/slot-machine.png";
import alpha from "../../assets/case-studies/opengov-coa/alpha.png";

const PLACEHOLDER_BODY =
  "I traveled with OpenGov account managers to visit local customers and test with live users. I used a short pre-interview script and conducted a standard task-based usability test. The goal was to assess whether users could understand what the interface was communicating and if they could successfully perform basic editing and navigation tasks. The results were very positive. Especially considering the unique approach to visualization, it was exciting to see how naturally users picked it up. These tests provided important validation of the concept, but we later learned there were limitations.";
---

<CaseStudyOverlay {...toOverlayProps(chartOfAccounts)}>
  <ProseBlock
    heading="Public servants"
    image={financeDirector}
    imageAlt="A finance director"
    fit="contain"
    imageElevation="image"
  >
    <p>
      OpenGov's primary users are the public servants who run local governments.
      This includes elected officials like mayors, but the more common
      day-to-day users are department heads, analysts, accountants, and finance
      directors. My work primarily focused on accounting and finance users.
      These individuals are risk-averse and detail-oriented, yet they take pride
      in their work, viewing it as their solemn mission to present financial
      data in a way that is understandable to their staff and the public.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <Banner
    title="Live prototype available"
    body="As an exercise, I rebuilt the CoA manager experience from memory, using Figma Make. Play around with it to get a sense of the interaction paradigm and what makes this product unique."
    href="https://www.figma.com/make/lDDxq1qeemMarlP2d465vp/-Prototype--COA-Manager-Rebuild?t=H5k7V31xVvAVXiV5-20&fullscreen=1"
    actionLabel="View AI prototype"
    image={livePrototype}
    imageAlt="The rebuilt chart of accounts manager prototype"
  />

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Financial transparency"
    layout="stacked"
    image={opengovReport}
    imageAlt="An OpenGov transparency report"
  >
    <p>
      Local governments upload large amounts of data to OpenGov on a quarterly
      or monthly basis, using the platform to share interactive reports both
      internally and publicly with the citizens they serve. Due to the
      complexity of financial data, the usefulness and accuracy of these reports
      heavily depend on how the data is categorized. Sophisticated
      categorization schemes are created in enterprise resource planning systems
      and must be mirrored in OpenGov. The categorization scheme for an
      organization is referred to as a "chart of accounts."
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Taxonomies"
    image={animalTaxonomy}
    imageAlt="A taxonomy of animals"
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      A chart of accounts is a long-standing, esoteric accounting tool,
      essentially a series of taxonomies. An easy analogy for the average person
      would be the classification of animals: just as scientists categorize
      animals into groups, accountants do the same for transactions. However, in
      accounting, multiple taxonomies are used for each transaction, and the
      structure can vary from one organization to another or even based on the
      type of report. A small government might use three taxonomies, each with
      2-3 levels, while more sophisticated governments could have 4-5 taxonomies
      with up to six levels. We often encountered charts that tracked tens of
      thousands of types.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="The legacy solution"
    layout="stacked"
    image={oldCoaEditor}
    imageAlt="The legacy chart of accounts editor"
  >
    <p>
      OpenGov already had an existing tool for managing charts of accounts,
      built as an MVP in the early days of the startup. The tool made certain
      implementation assumptions that required these taxonomies to be handled
      separately. It also used a folder-like interface that did not accurately
      represent the data and, due to a lack of error handling, allowed users to
      make nonsensical edits and accidentally corrupt their data. When I was
      assigned to redesign the chart of accounts manager, there were aspects we
      couldn't change, but we could improve the user interface.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Listening and learning">
    <p>
      Aside from a background in math and a curiosity for data structures, I had
      little relevant experience for this project. My first step was to listen
      and learn. Luckily, we had retired finance directors on staff with decades
      of experience, as well as a team of technical support staff who interacted
      with customers daily, training them and solving technical challenges. I
      set up learning sessions and made countless desk visits. I also took the
      opportunity to join customer calls to learn directly from customers,
      listening for the kinds of questions they asked customer success and then
      asking questions of my own.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Becoming an expert"
    image={diagrams}
    imageAlt="Diagrams explaining chart of accounts concepts"
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      Given the complexity of this tool and the data it managed, I had to
      grapple with the subject matter. I began diagramming concepts, initially
      just to help articulate ideas to myself. I shared these diagrams with the
      customer success teams for feedback, prompting great discussions. We also
      realized these documents could serve as training tools, as I was not the
      only team member struggling to learn these concepts. Ultimately, the
      customer success team began distributing these documents to customers as
      well. Over the course of this project, and in my later work, I became one
      of the company's chart of accounts experts.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Early concepts"
    image={earlyConcept}
    imageAlt="An early dendrogram concept"
  >
    <p>
      Once I felt I was beginning to grasp the concept of a chart of accounts, I
      looked for ways to better represent it in the interface. Inspired by a
      popular data visualization library, I mocked up an interface that
      represented taxonomies as a dendrogram. It was an exciting approach,
      offering an unprecedented bird's-eye view of the chart of accounts.
      However, there were serious problems. First, the tree-like organization
      made it difficult to view the entire categorization for a type—something
      critically important in a taxonomy. Second, illustrating the connections
      meant that text was not packed tightly, making it hard to see thousands of
      nodes in a readable way.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Back to the drawing board"
    image={artifact}
    imageAlt="A customer spreadsheet used for artifact analysis"
  >
    <p>
      One of my favorite, underappreciated research methods is artifact
      analysis. By examining the files and objects that people use to perform
      their work, you can gain valuable, tacit insights. I analyzed the
      spreadsheet files that users were uploading to OpenGov, trying to
      understand how information was laid out. I realized that spreadsheets did
      a great job of displaying taxonomies in a compact, readable, and
      easy-to-manipulate way. The only drawback was that the row-column grid
      required labels to be repeated when many categories were grouped under a
      parent, leading to visual noise. It occurred to me: what if we could
      create something dynamic?
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A new concept"
    layout="stacked"
    image={slotMachine}
    imageAlt="The slot machine concept"
  >
    <p>
      Imagining a dynamic spreadsheet with columns automatically scrolling to
      show relationships, I mocked up the new concept. It was a challenging idea
      to communicate abstractly, so I tried various methods to explain it,
      including Framer, animations, and even simple HTML/CSS prototypes. The
      concept became known as the “slot machine” due to the way it synchronized.
      Eventually, I paired with an engineer who built an early alpha version for
      testing.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Usability testing">
    <p>{PLACEHOLDER_BODY}</p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Working with Customer Success">
    <p>{PLACEHOLDER_BODY}</p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Impact">
    <p>{PLACEHOLDER_BODY}</p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <AssetRow
    assets={[
      { image: alpha, alt: "The alpha build in testing" },
      { image: livePrototype, alt: "The rebuilt prototype" },
    ]}
  />
</CaseStudyOverlay>
